1740-### 30.3 Cancellation token (frozen)
1741-
1742-```python
1743-class CancellationToken:
1744-    def is_cancelled(self) -> bool: ...
1745-    def raise_if_cancelled(self) -> None:   # raises ProviderCancelled
1746-    def on_cancel(self, callback: Callable[[], None]) -> None:   # best-effort, idempotent
1747-```
1748-
1749-- Backed by the scheduler's existing `stop_requested` callable (`scheduler.py:421`,
1750-  `:708`); the token is a wrapper, not a new mechanism.
1751-- **Cooperative only.** No thread is killed and no process is signalled. A provider that
1752-  declares the `cancel` capability (§20.4) must poll `is_cancelled()` at least every 5 s
1753-  during any wait, and must return or raise within 10 s of the flag being set.
1754-- A provider that does **not** declare `cancel` is not cancelled mid-call; the platform stops
1755-  after the call returns (`scheduler.py:720-721`). This is exactly today's behavior and is why
1756-  all five provider-backed node types currently declare `"cancel": False`
1757-  (`registry.py:158, 197, 243, 267, 296`).
1758-- Cancellation is **not** an error condition to be retried. It maps to `ProviderCancelled` →
1759-  workflow code `CANCELLED` → node status `cancelled` (`scheduler.py:754-766`), never to
1760-  `failed`.
1761-- `on_cancel` callbacks run once, on the thread that first observes cancellation through
1762-  `is_cancelled()` / `raise_if_cancelled()`. The existing scheduler exposes only
1763-  `threading.Event.is_set` as a callable, so a callback cannot run on the thread that calls
1764-  `Event.set` without inventing a second cancellation mechanism. Callbacks must not block or
1765-  raise; exceptions are logged and swallowed.
